209
00:10:30.931 --> 00:10:31.848
Part 4.

210
00:10:32.128 --> 00:10:34.005
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

211
00:10:35.255 --> 00:10:37.687
Journey 1: from app open to match start.

212
00:10:37.967 --> 00:10:38.927
App open.

213
00:10:39.207 --> 00:10:47.421
The client checks its version against remote config and downloads updated content (maps, skins) from the CDN in the background.

214
00:10:47.701 --> 00:10:54.079
It logs in with a device ID, phone OTP or a platform account, and receives a session token.

215
00:10:54.359 --> 00:10:56.258
Presence connects over a WebSocket.

216
00:10:56.538 --> 00:10:57.477
Home screen.

217
00:10:58.827 --> 00:11:04.587
The profile, inventory, battle pass progress, events (from live ops) and store offers load.

218
00:11:04.867 --> 00:11:07.320
Offers may be personalised through experiments.

219
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.475
Party up.

220
00:11:08.755 --> 00:11:12.723
Friends accept an invite and a party is formed in the social service.

221
00:11:13.003 --> 00:11:15.179
The party leader chooses a mode and queues.

222
00:11:15.459 --> 00:11:19.725
The whole party enters matchmaking as one ticket with the members' ratings.

223
00:11:21.075 --> 00:11:22.313
Latency probing.

224
00:11:22.593 --> 00:11:29.569
The client pings a set of region endpoints (quality-of-service beacons) and reports RTTs to the matchmaker.

225
00:11:29.849 --> 00:11:33.241
Candidate regions are those under the latency threshold.

226
00:11:33.521 --> 00:11:35.761
Matchmaking (§6.2).

227
00:11:36.041 --> 00:11:38.771
The ticket enters queues for eligible regions.

228
00:11:39.051 --> 00:11:47.798
The matchmaker groups tickets into a balanced match: similar ratings, parties matched to parties where possible, and team balance by total rating.

229
00:11:48.078 --> 00:11:51.619
The acceptable skill range widens as wait time grows.

230
00:11:52.969 --> 00:11:53.972
Allocation.

231
00:11:54.252 --> 00:12:02.081
Once the match is formed, the allocator requests a ready game server in the chosen region from the fleet manager, which keeps a warm buffer.

232
00:12:02.361 --> 00:12:09.337
The server gets the match config (map, mode, player IDs, teams) and one-time join tokens for each player.

233
00:12:09.617 --> 00:12:10.364
Connect.

234
00:12:10.644 --> 00:12:15.892
Clients receive the server IP:port and a join token, and connect over UDP.

235
00:12:17.242 --> 00:12:26.095
The server validates tokens, loads players' loadouts from progression (or from the match config) and starts the pre-game lobby, where players drop in.

236
00:12:27.445 --> 00:12:29.792
Journey 2: inside a match, the game loop.

237
00:12:30.072 --> 00:12:31.117
Client input.

238
00:12:31.397 --> 00:12:42.683
Each client frame (60 fps), the client samples input (movement, aim, fire) and sends input commands to the server, tagged with a sequence number and client timestamp.

239
00:12:42.963 --> 00:12:46.397
Batches of inputs are sent 30 to 60 times per second.

240
00:12:46.677 --> 00:12:47.808
Client prediction.

241
00:12:48.088 --> 00:12:54.893
The client applies its own inputs immediately to its local copy of its own character, so movement feels instant.

242
00:12:55.173 --> 00:12:58.096
It keeps a history of inputs not yet acknowledged.

243
00:12:58.376 --> 00:12:59.336
Server tick.

244
00:12:59.616 --> 00:13:01.941
At 30 Hz the authoritative server:

245
00:13:03.291 --> 00:13:05.041
Collects inputs from all players.

246
00:13:05.321 --> 00:13:11.763
Simulates movement with the game's physics and validates it (speed limits, collisions, no walking through walls).

247
00:13:12.043 --> 00:13:16.011
Processes shots with lag compensation (§6.4).

248
00:13:16.291 --> 00:13:18.873
Applies damage, deaths and item pickups.

249
00:13:19.153 --> 00:13:20.753
Updates the storm or zone.

250
00:13:21.033 --> 00:13:22.675
Produces the new world state.

251
00:13:22.955 --> 00:13:24.406
Snapshots to clients.

252
00:13:25.756 --> 00:13:35.612
The server sends each client a snapshot of relevant state: its own authoritative position and entities in its area of interest (§6.5).

253
00:13:35.892 --> 00:13:39.903
Snapshots are delta-compressed against the last acknowledged snapshot.

254
00:13:40.183 --> 00:13:41.420
Reconciliation.

255
00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:49.295
When the client receives the authoritative state for its own character, it rewinds to that state and replays unacknowledged inputs.

256
00:13:49.575 --> 00:13:54.119
Small differences are smoothed; big ones snap (the player feels rubber-banding).

257
00:13:55.469 --> 00:13:56.855
Other players' rendering.

258
00:13:57.135 --> 00:14:07.759
Clients render other players slightly in the past (for example, 100 ms interpolation buffer) between two snapshots, for smooth movement despite packet jitter.

259
00:14:08.039 --> 00:14:09.319
Hit registration.

260
00:14:09.599 --> 00:14:15.722
When a player fires, the client may show hit effects immediately (optimistic), but the server decides.

261
00:14:16.002 --> 00:14:19.970
Lag compensation rewinds other players to where the shooter saw them.

262
00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:22.792
Events and telemetry.

263
00:14:23.072 --> 00:14:28.149
Kills, deaths, damage, item use and positions (sampled) are streamed to telemetry.

264
00:14:28.429 --> 00:14:31.075
Suspicious patterns are flagged for anti-cheat.

265
00:14:32.425 --> 00:14:35.475
Journey 3: match end, rewards and leaderboards.

266
00:14:35.755 --> 00:14:36.758
Match ends.

267
00:14:37.038 --> 00:14:42.307
The server computes results: placement, kills, damage, survival time, team outcome.

268
00:14:42.587 --> 00:14:45.425
It sends a signed match result to the session service.

269
00:14:45.705 --> 00:14:46.814
Session service.

270
00:14:47.094 --> 00:14:53.046
Validates the result: signature, known server, match ID not already processed (idempotency).

271
00:14:53.326 --> 00:14:55.289
Publishes a MatchCompleted event.

272
00:14:56.639 --> 00:15:00.777
Consumers (asynchronous, each idempotent by match ID and player):

273
00:15:01.057 --> 00:15:06.860
Ratings: update each player's skill rating (§6.1) and ranked points or tier.

274
00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:08.057
Progression:

275
00:15:08.337 --> 00:15:11.281
XP, battle pass progress, mission completions.

276
00:15:11.561 --> 00:15:16.511
Economy: rewards such as soft currency and loot drops, credited through the ledger.

277
00:15:16.791 --> 00:15:20.759
Leaderboards: update seasonal scores (§6.6).

278
00:15:21.039 --> 00:15:25.668
Anti-cheat: statistical checks on the performance (§6.7).

279
00:15:27.018 --> 00:15:29.749
Analytics: the match record goes to the lakehouse.

280
00:15:30.029 --> 00:15:31.095
Client summary.

281
00:15:31.375 --> 00:15:39.247
The client shows XP gained, rank change and rewards, typically within 1 to 3 s via a push over the realtime gateway.

282
00:15:39.527 --> 00:15:40.615
Server teardown.

283
00:15:40.895 --> 00:15:46.975
The game server uploads a replay (if recorded), cleans up and returns to the fleet (or is recycled).

284
00:15:48.325 --> 00:15:50.437
Journey 4: buying the battle pass.

285
00:15:50.717 --> 00:15:54.067
The player taps Buy Battle Pass (950 gems).

286
00:15:54.347 --> 00:16:08.640
They have 500 gems, so they buy a gem pack for 449 rupeesthrough the app store (Google Play or App Store billing) or a payment gateway on PC (UPI or cards; VERIFY store policies for India).

287
00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.141
The store completes payment and the client receives a receipt.

288
00:16:12.421 --> 00:16:15.237
The client sends the receipt to the economy service:

289
00:16:15.517 --> 00:16:18.696
The service verifies it with the store's server API.

290
00:16:18.976 --> 00:16:24.736
It checks the transaction ID has not been used before (idempotency, stopping replayed receipts).

291
00:16:26.086 --> 00:16:31.355
It credits gems in the ledger: debit store revenue clearing, credit player gems wallet.

292
00:16:31.635 --> 00:16:33.427
The player buys the battle pass.

293
00:16:33.707 --> 00:16:42.155
The economy service atomically: debits 950 gems from the wallet;. grants the battle pass entitlement in inventory.

294
00:16:42.435 --> 00:16:46.489
This happens in one transaction, or as a saga with compensation.

295
00:16:47.839 --> 00:16:48.692
Refunds.

296
00:16:48.972 --> 00:16:53.196
Store notifications (refund or chargeback) arrive through webhooks.

297
00:16:53.476 --> 00:16:58.959
The service claws back currency, going negative if already spent (policy), and flags abuse.

298
00:16:59.239 --> 00:17:00.028
Audit.

299
00:17:00.308 --> 00:17:09.481
Every currency movement is a ledger entry with a reason, which supports support-team investigations and finance reconciliation with store reports.

300
00:17:10.831 --> 00:17:13.221
Journey 5: a cheater and the ban wave.

301
00:17:13.501 --> 00:17:15.143
A player uses an aimbot.

302
00:17:15.423 --> 00:17:21.887
It reads game memory, and the client-side anti-cheat module may detect known signatures or tampering.

303
00:17:22.167 --> 00:17:33.538
Server-side, statistical signals appear: inhumanly fast target acquisition;. headshot rate far above the rating norm;. tracking through walls;. impossible reaction times.

304
00:17:33.818 --> 00:17:38.319
The anti-cheat model (§6.7) scores the player per match.

305
00:17:39.669 --> 00:17:41.419
Player reports add evidence.

306
00:17:41.699 --> 00:17:44.536
A replay or kill-cam clip goes to a review queue.

307
00:17:44.816 --> 00:17:45.605
Action.

308
00:17:45.885 --> 00:17:49.405
High-confidence detection leads to the account being flagged.

309
00:17:49.685 --> 00:17:55.061
Bans are often held for a ban wave, so cheat makers don't learn which change caused detection.

310
00:17:55.341 --> 00:17:59.181
Devices or hardware IDs may be banned for repeat offenders.

311
00:17:59.461 --> 00:18:03.472
Affected matches may have rating changes rolled back for victims.

312
00:18:04.822 --> 00:18:07.595
Appeals go through a support process with evidence.

313
00:18:08.945 --> 00:18:10.716
Journey 6: a season launch.

314
00:18:10.996 --> 00:18:12.063
Weeks before.

315
00:18:12.343 --> 00:18:18.039
New content (map, skins, battle pass) is built and delivered via the CDN in a patch.

316
00:18:18.319 --> 00:18:22.351
Pre-download is enabled days before, which spreads the bandwidth load.

317
00:18:22.631 --> 00:18:23.612
Launch hour.

318
00:18:23.892 --> 00:18:26.921
Remote config flips the season at a scheduled time.

319
00:18:27.201 --> 00:18:31.724
Ranked tiers soft-reset (§6.1) and new leaderboards open.

320
00:18:32.004 --> 00:18:35.759
CCU spikes 2 to 3 times as lapsed players return.

321
00:18:37.109 --> 00:18:38.069
Capacity.

322
00:18:38.349 --> 00:18:43.149
The fleet is pre-scaled with extra warm servers per region, and matchmakers scale.

323
00:18:43.429 --> 00:18:50.170
The login service has queueing (You are 12,340 in queue) to protect the backend if needed.

324
00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:51.325
Monitoring.

325
00:18:51.605 --> 00:19:01.482
Watch queue times, match start failures, crash rates on new content, store purchase success and economy anomalies (an exploit granting infinite currency).

326
00:19:01.762 --> 00:19:04.642
Kill switches exist for features and store items.

327
00:19:05.992 --> 00:19:06.952
Part 5.

328
00:19:07.232 --> 00:19:08.768
Core Subsystems in Depth.

329
00:19:10.018 --> 00:19:11.682
Game server fleet management.

330
00:19:11.962 --> 00:19:13.178
Fleet per region.

331
00:19:13.458 --> 00:19:19.751
Pools of VMs or bare-metal machines, each running several game server processes (containers).

332
00:19:20.031 --> 00:19:20.863
States:

333
00:19:21.143 --> 00:19:24.685
Ready (warm, waiting), Allocated (match assigned), Shutdown.

334
00:19:24.965 --> 00:19:26.309
Buffer autoscaling.

335
00:19:26.589 --> 00:19:33.010
Keep a buffer of Ready servers (for example, 10 to 20% or a fixed count) per region and mode.

336
00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:35.445
Scale nodes up as the buffer depletes.

337
00:19:35.725 --> 00:19:38.306
Scale down by draining nodes without matches.

338
00:19:39.656 --> 00:19:41.832
Never kill a node with active matches.

339
00:19:42.112 --> 00:19:43.392
Hybrid capacity.

340
00:19:43.672 --> 00:19:54.765
Bare metal or reserved instances for baseline load, cloud VMs for peaks, and spot instances only for short matches or with graceful handling (VERIFY cost trade-offs).

341
00:19:55.045 --> 00:19:55.877
Tooling.

342
00:19:56.157 --> 00:20:06.483
Kubernetes + Agones (open source), or managed services such as GameLift or PlayFab Multiplayer Servers (VERIFY product names and capabilities).

343
00:20:07.833 --> 00:20:08.537
Health.

344
00:20:08.817 --> 00:20:11.355
Servers report tick timing and player count.

345
00:20:11.635 --> 00:20:20.638
The allocator avoids unhealthy nodes, and crashed matches notify the session service to compensate players (for example, no rank loss).

346
00:20:21.988 --> 00:20:23.311
Matchmaking service.

347
00:20:23.591 --> 00:20:24.423
Tickets.

348
00:20:24.703 --> 00:20:31.231
(ticket ID, player IDs, mode, ratings, party size, region RTTs, preferences, created time).

349
00:20:31.511 --> 00:20:32.385
Pools.

350
00:20:32.665 --> 00:20:34.841
Per (region, mode, maybe platform).

351
00:20:35.121 --> 00:20:38.236
Tickets can be in several regional pools until matched.

352
00:20:38.516 --> 00:20:39.583
Match function.

353
00:20:39.863 --> 00:20:46.561
Runs every ~1 s over each pool and forms matches that meet the constraints (§6.2).

354
00:20:46.841 --> 00:20:47.823
Frameworks:

355
00:20:48.103 --> 00:20:53.457
Open Match (open source) separates the match function from the infrastructure (VERIFY).

356
00:20:53.737 --> 00:20:54.569
Backfill.

357
00:20:54.849 --> 00:20:57.985
Replace leavers in ongoing matches (casual modes).

358
00:20:59.335 --> 00:21:00.914
Low-population handling.

359
00:21:01.194 --> 00:21:10.346
Widen skill ranges, merge regions, add bots for casual modes (commonly done in mobile BR games; VERIFY for any specific title).

360
00:21:11.696 --> 00:21:13.829
Netcode and the authoritative server.

361
00:21:14.109 --> 00:21:15.624
The server holds the truth.

362
00:21:15.904 --> 00:21:21.728
Clients send inputs, not state (I pressed forward, not I am at x,y), so they can't teleport.

363
00:21:22.008 --> 00:21:23.032
Validation.

364
00:21:23.312 --> 00:21:28.773
Movement speed, fire rate, ammo, cooldowns and line-of-sight are all checked on the server.

365
00:21:29.053 --> 00:21:30.931
Reliability over UDP.

366
00:21:31.211 --> 00:21:34.325
Unreliable channel for snapshots (latest wins).

367
00:21:34.605 --> 00:21:39.405
Reliable-ordered channel for important events (chat, item purchases in-match).

368
00:21:40.755 --> 00:21:42.910
ACK bitfields in packet headers.

369
00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:44.427
Bandwidth control.

370
00:21:44.707 --> 00:21:54.073
Delta compression, quantisation (positions to cm precision, angles to 16 bits), interest management, and priority for nearby entities.

371
00:21:54.353 --> 00:21:55.334
Tick budget.

372
00:21:55.614 --> 00:21:57.043
Profile the server loop.

373
00:21:57.323 --> 00:22:04.171
At 100 players, physics, hit detection and replication must fit inside 33 ms with headroom.

374
00:22:04.451 --> 00:22:06.670
Spatial partitioning speeds up queries.

375
00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:09.215
Economy ledger.

376
00:22:09.495 --> 00:22:10.455
Double-entry.

377
00:22:10.735 --> 00:22:17.903
Every movement has matching debit and credit entries, per currency: hard (purchased) gems and soft (earned) coins.

378
00:22:18.183 --> 00:22:26.161
Hard currency may need separate accounting of paid versus free portions, for revenue recognition and regulations (VERIFY).

379
00:22:26.441 --> 00:22:27.721
Idempotency keys.

380
00:22:28.001 --> 00:22:31.372
Store transaction IDs, match IDs and grant IDs.

381
00:22:31.652 --> 00:22:33.401
Retries never double-grant.

382
00:22:33.681 --> 00:22:34.620
Inventory.

383
00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:41.620
Entitlements such as skins and battle pass tiers are recorded with their source (purchase, reward, gift) and time.

384
00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:43.244
Exploit containment.

385
00:22:44.594 --> 00:22:49.927
Anomaly detection on currency velocity (an account earning 100 times normal).

386
00:22:50.207 --> 00:22:51.381
Per-account limits.

387
00:22:51.661 --> 00:22:52.770
Rollback tooling.

388
00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:55.144
Leaderboards.

389
00:22:55.424 --> 00:23:04.704
Types: global;. regional or country;. friends;. clan;. seasonal (reset each season) versus lifetime;. event-specific (time-boxed).

390
00:23:06.054 --> 00:23:14.331
Operations: update a score (after a match);. get the top N;. get my rank;. get neighbours (players around me);. get friends' ranks.

391
00:23:14.611 --> 00:23:17.491
Implementation: see §6.6.

392
00:23:18.841 --> 00:23:20.484
Social, presence and chat.

393
00:23:20.764 --> 00:23:21.596
Presence.

394
00:23:21.876 --> 00:23:25.844
Heartbeats over WebSocket to a presence store (Redis with TTL).

395
00:23:26.124 --> 00:23:27.980
Friends subscribe to changes.

396
00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:29.113
Parties.

397
00:23:29.393 --> 00:23:32.657
A small state machine (invite, join, leave, leader).

398
00:23:32.937 --> 00:23:36.756
Party state is kept on a party service shard and pushed to members.

399
00:23:37.036 --> 00:23:37.740
Chat.

400
00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:44.505
Similar to Ch 41, with moderation, filters and reporting, plus stricter settings for minors.

401
00:23:44.785 --> 00:23:45.575
Voice.

402
00:23:45.855 --> 00:23:52.831
In-match team voice is carried by the game server or a separate voice SFU (Vivox-style services; VERIFY).

403
00:23:53.111 --> 00:23:56.012
Features include push-to-talk and mute and report.

404
00:23:57.362 --> 00:23:59.111
Live ops and remote config.

405
00:23:59.391 --> 00:24:00.479
Config service.

406
00:24:00.759 --> 00:24:10.701
Versioned JSON configs (drop rates, event schedules, balance tweaks, store layout), targeted by segment (country, platform, app version, experiment arm).

407
00:24:10.981 --> 00:24:12.943
Clients fetch at login and on push.

408
00:24:13.223 --> 00:24:15.762
Config reaches game servers at match start.

409
00:24:16.042 --> 00:24:17.087
Experiments.

410
00:24:17.367 --> 00:24:24.983
A or B tests for store offers, onboarding and matchmaking parameters, with guardrail metrics (retention, match quality).

411
00:24:25.263 --> 00:24:26.266
Kill switches.

412
00:24:26.546 --> 00:24:29.917
For buggy items, exploits, or modes causing crashes.

413
00:24:31.267 --> 00:24:32.205
Part 6.

414
00:24:32.485 --> 00:24:33.445
Algorithms.

415
00:24:34.695 --> 00:24:36.039
Skill rating systems.

416
00:24:36.319 --> 00:24:37.066
Elo.

417
00:24:37.346 --> 00:24:43.895
Expected score EA = 1 / (1 + 10^((RB minus RA)/400)).

418
00:24:44.175 --> 00:24:47.695
After a match, RA ← RA + K(SA minus EA).

419
00:24:47.975 --> 00:24:50.194
Simple, and designed for 1v1.

420
00:24:50.474 --> 00:24:51.946
Glicko or Glicko-2.

421
00:24:52.226 --> 00:24:55.277
Adds rating deviation (RD), the uncertainty.

422
00:24:55.557 --> 00:24:59.653
New or returning players have high RD, so their ratings move fast.

423
00:24:59.933 --> 00:25:02.749
RD shrinks with play and grows with inactivity.

424
00:25:04.099 --> 00:25:06.083
Glicko-2 adds volatility.

425
00:25:06.363 --> 00:25:09.093
TrueSkill (published by Microsoft Research).

426
00:25:09.373 --> 00:25:12.765
Bayesian: each player has skill ~ N(μ, σ²).

427
00:25:13.045 --> 00:25:17.547
Supports teams and multi-player free-for-all (placements in a battle royale).

428
00:25:17.827 --> 00:25:20.600
Updates via factor graphs and message passing.

429
00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:24.208
The displayed conservative skill is μ minus 3σ.

430
00:25:25.558 --> 00:25:31.851
TrueSkill 2 adds factors such as individual performance and quitting (publicly described; VERIFY).

431
00:25:32.131 --> 00:25:33.795
Battle royale adaptation.

432
00:25:34.075 --> 00:25:40.625
Treat placement as a ranking of teams, and update pairwise or with a TrueSkill-style multi-team update.

433
00:25:40.905 --> 00:25:42.825
Kills may add a performance factor.

434
00:25:43.105 --> 00:25:45.345
Visible rank versus hidden MMR.

435
00:25:46.695 --> 00:25:52.519
Ranked tiers (Bronze, then Diamond) are points-based for motivation, with win streak bonuses.

436
00:25:52.799 --> 00:25:55.316
The hidden MMR is used for matchmaking.

437
00:25:55.596 --> 00:26:00.844
At season start, soft-reset visible ranks (compress toward the mean) and raise the RD.

438
00:26:02.194 --> 00:26:03.687
Matchmaking algorithm.

439
00:26:03.967 --> 00:26:24.127
Goal: form matches that minimise a cost combining: skill disparity (within and across teams);. latency (all players under the threshold to the chosen region);. wait time (a penalty that grows as players wait);. party composition (premades versus solos);. other constraints (platform, input device, language).

440
00:26:24.407 --> 00:26:25.602
Typical approach:

441
00:26:26.952 --> 00:26:28.424
Sort the pool by rating.

442
00:26:28.704 --> 00:26:31.541
Candidate groups come from windows around each ticket.

443
00:26:31.821 --> 00:26:45.197
Each ticket's acceptable window expands over time, for example, ±50 MMR at 0 s, ±150 at 30 s, ±300 at 60 s.

444
00:26:45.477 --> 00:26:50.533
Greedily form matches starting from the oldest tickets, which guards against starvation.

445
00:26:50.813 --> 00:26:56.509
For team balance, assign players to teams to minimise the difference in total (or mean) skill.

446
00:26:56.789 --> 00:27:04.853
This is a partitioning heuristic: sort by skill and alternate snake-draft (1 to 2-2 to 1), or run a small search.

447
00:27:06.203 --> 00:27:07.270
Battle royale:

448
00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:11.390
100 players is a large match, so skill matching is looser.

449
00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:15.595
Use broad skill brackets, fill by time, and region is critical.

450
00:27:15.875 --> 00:27:25.454
Evaluation: measure predicted match quality (for example, the TrueSkill draw probability) against actual outcomes, and queue times by skill band.

451
00:27:25.734 --> 00:27:27.377
Top players wait longest.

452
00:27:28.727 --> 00:27:31.009
Client prediction and reconciliation.

453
00:27:31.289 --> 00:27:32.185
Prediction.

454
00:27:32.465 --> 00:27:36.156
The client runs the same movement code locally on its inputs.

455
00:27:36.436 --> 00:27:41.556
The server's authoritative state arrives later with the last processed input sequence number.

456
00:27:41.836 --> 00:27:43.073
Reconciliation.

457
00:27:43.353 --> 00:27:46.233
Set the local state to the server state at sequence N.

458
00:27:46.513 --> 00:27:48.881
Re-apply inputs N+1 onwards.

459
00:27:49.161 --> 00:27:55.177
If the result differs from the displayed state by a small error, smooth over a few frames; otherwise snap.

460
00:27:56.527 --> 00:27:57.978
Entity interpolation.

461
00:27:58.258 --> 00:28:07.474
Other players are rendered by interpolating between buffered snapshots at a render time slightly behind the latest (for example, 2 snapshot intervals).

462
00:28:07.754 --> 00:28:12.533
This handles jitter; extrapolation is used only briefly on packet loss.

463
00:28:13.883 --> 00:28:15.205
Lag compensation.

464
00:28:15.485 --> 00:28:21.672
Problem: the shooter sees enemies where they were ~RTT or 2 + interpolation delay ago.

465
00:28:21.952 --> 00:28:24.725
Without compensation, players have to aim ahead.

466
00:28:25.005 --> 00:28:29.784
Server-side rewind (publicly described for Source engine games; VERIFY):

467
00:28:30.064 --> 00:28:34.736
The server keeps a history of each player's hitboxes for the last ~1 s.

468
00:28:35.016 --> 00:28:43.165
When a shot command arrives, it estimates the time the shooter saw (current time minus shooter latency minus interpolation delay).

469
00:28:43.445 --> 00:28:48.779
It rewinds other players' hitboxes to that time, performs the ray cast and restores them.

470
00:28:50.129 --> 00:28:52.006
Trade-off: shot behind cover.

471
00:28:52.286 --> 00:28:57.193
The victim reaches cover on their screen but still dies, because the shooter saw them earlier.

472
00:28:57.473 --> 00:29:03.062
Cap the rewind (for example, 200 ms) to limit the advantage of high-ping players.

473
00:29:03.342 --> 00:29:06.222
Rollback netcode (fighting games, 1v1):

474
00:29:06.502 --> 00:29:09.254
Clients simulate with predicted remote inputs.

475
00:29:09.534 --> 00:29:13.331
When the actual inputs arrive, they roll back and re-simulate frames.

476
00:29:14.681 --> 00:29:21.401
This needs deterministic simulation and fast re-simulation (GGPO-style; publicly described).

477
00:29:22.751 --> 00:29:23.989
Interest management.

478
00:29:24.269 --> 00:29:25.143
Problem:

479
00:29:25.423 --> 00:29:28.410
100 players times 1000s of entities.

480
00:29:28.690 --> 00:29:33.746
Sending everything to everyone is too much bandwidth, and it leaks information to wallhacks.

481
00:29:34.026 --> 00:29:35.349
Spatial partitioning.

482
00:29:35.629 --> 00:29:37.399
A grid or quadtree over the map.

483
00:29:37.679 --> 00:29:43.034
Each client receives entities in nearby cells, with priority by distance and relevance.

484
00:29:43.314 --> 00:29:44.509
Visibility culling.

485
00:29:44.789 --> 00:29:48.586
Don't send enemies the client cannot possibly see (occlusion checks).

486
00:29:48.866 --> 00:29:51.447
This reduces what wallhack cheats can reveal.

487
00:29:51.727 --> 00:29:53.754
Update frequency by priority.

488
00:29:54.034 --> 00:29:57.853
Nearby enemies every tick; distant vehicles every few ticks.

489
00:29:59.203 --> 00:30:00.845
Leaderboard ranking at scale.

490
00:30:01.125 --> 00:30:09.083
Redis sorted sets (ZADD or ZREVRANK or ZREVRANGE) handle the top-N and rank for millions of entries in O(log N).

491
00:30:09.363 --> 00:30:15.507
One sorted set with 20M members is feasible on a large node (memory ~ a few GB).

492
00:30:15.787 --> 00:30:17.643
It is a hot key for writes, though.

493
00:30:17.923 --> 00:30:19.565
Scaling beyond one node.

494
00:30:19.845 --> 00:30:21.360
Shard by score ranges.

495
00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:26.013
Keep a small count index (how many players are in each shard or score bucket).

496
00:30:26.293 --> 00:30:30.219
Rank = (players in higher buckets) + (rank within the bucket).

497
00:30:31.569 --> 00:30:33.553
Updates move a player between buckets.

498
00:30:33.833 --> 00:30:55.358
Or shard by player hash, with approximate global rank: maintain a histogram of scores (bucket counts) globally, updated incrementally;. rank about sum of counts above the player's score, plus an estimate within the bucket;. show exact ranks only for the top 10k (a separate exact sorted set) and percentiles for everyone else (Top 3%).

499
00:30:56.708 --> 00:30:58.009
Friends leaderboards.

500
00:30:58.289 --> 00:31:02.151
Fetch friends' scores directly (a small N) and sort per request.

501
00:31:02.431 --> 00:31:04.415
Cache per user for a short TTL.

502
00:31:04.695 --> 00:31:05.953
Seasonal resets.

503
00:31:06.233 --> 00:31:08.751
Create a new board keyed by season ID.

504
00:31:09.031 --> 00:31:10.844
Archive the old one to storage.

505
00:31:11.124 --> 00:31:11.956
Ties.

506
00:31:12.236 --> 00:31:20.961
Encode the tie-break in the score, for example, score times 10^10 + (MAXTS minus achieved time), so earlier achievers rank higher.

507
00:31:21.241 --> 00:31:22.137
Integrity.

508
00:31:22.417 --> 00:31:25.020
Only server-validated results update boards.

509
00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:27.177
Anti-cheat can remove entries.

510
00:31:28.527 --> 00:31:29.914
Anti-cheat detection.

511
00:31:30.194 --> 00:31:31.026
Layers:

512
00:31:31.306 --> 00:31:38.047
Prevention by design: server authority, input validation, interest management (don't send hidden enemies).

513
00:31:38.327 --> 00:31:51.021
Client integrity: anti-tamper and obfuscation;. integrity checks;. detection of known cheat software signatures, debuggers, emulators and rooted devices (with care for false positives);.

514
00:31:52.371 --> 00:31:58.195
on PC, kernel-level anti-cheat is used by some titles (controversial; VERIFY).

515
00:31:58.475 --> 00:32:13.152
Server-side statistics: features per player per match: headshot ratio;. reaction time (time from an enemy becoming visible to aim on target);. aim snap angles;. accuracy versus distance;. tracking through occluders;.

516
00:32:14.502 --> 00:32:18.001
kill or death ratio versus MMR expectations.

517
00:32:18.281 --> 00:32:25.790
Models (gradient-boosted trees, sequence models over aim trajectories) score deviation from human distributions.

518
00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:30.337
Reports and review: weight reports by the reporter's accuracy history.

519
00:32:30.617 --> 00:32:39.726
Response policy: confidence thresholds, delayed ban waves, shadow pools (cheaters matched with cheaters), hardware or device bans, and appeals.

520
00:32:41.076 --> 00:32:43.188
Random rewards and drop fairness.

521
00:32:43.468 --> 00:32:49.591
Loot drop tables with configured probabilities, served by the server (never computed on the client).

522
00:32:49.871 --> 00:32:58.297
Pity systems: a guarantee within N attempts (a counter per player), or increasing probability after failures (bad luck protection).

523
00:32:58.577 --> 00:33:06.876
Disclosure: publish odds where required, and consider regulations on paid random items (loot boxes) by country (VERIFY).

524
00:33:07.156 --> 00:33:12.361
Auditability: log RNG seeds and outcomes per grant for dispute resolution.

