00:41:05.548 --> 00:41:06.892
Interview Q and A.

715
00:41:08.142 --> 00:41:08.974
Beginner.

716
00:41:09.254 --> 00:41:10.278
Question 1.

717
00:41:10.558 --> 00:41:14.803
Why do competitive online games use authoritative dedicated servers?

718
00:41:15.083 --> 00:41:16.449
Thirty-second answer:

719
00:41:16.729 --> 00:41:29.123
The server holds the true game state and validates every input, so clients can't cheat by sending fake positions or damage; it also removes host advantage and NAT problems of peer-to-peer.

720
00:41:30.473 --> 00:41:31.476
Question 2.

721
00:41:31.756 --> 00:41:34.487
How would you build a leaderboard for a million players?

722
00:41:34.767 --> 00:41:36.132
Thirty-second answer:

723
00:41:36.412 --> 00:41:38.439
A Redis sorted set keyed by season:

724
00:41:38.719 --> 00:41:48.617
ZADD to update after each match, ZREVRANGE for the top N, ZREVRANK for my rank, with snapshots to storage, only server-validated scores update it.

725
00:41:49.967 --> 00:41:51.013
Question 3.

726
00:41:51.293 --> 00:41:54.279
Why do games use UDP instead of TCP?

727
00:41:54.559 --> 00:41:55.925
Thirty-second answer:

728
00:41:56.205 --> 00:42:10.455
Real-time state is time-sensitive, a lost old snapshot is useless once a newer one arrives; TCP would block newer data behind retransmissions; games add their own reliability only for messages that need it.

729
00:42:11.805 --> 00:42:12.872
Question 4.

730
00:42:13.152 --> 00:42:15.797
What is Elo and how does it update after a match?

731
00:42:16.077 --> 00:42:17.443
Thirty-second answer:

732
00:42:17.723 --> 00:42:30.395
Each player has a rating; the expected result against an opponent comes from the rating difference; after the match, rating moves by K times (actual minus expected), so upsets move ratings more.

733
00:42:31.745 --> 00:42:32.747
Intermediate.

734
00:42:33.027 --> 00:42:34.137
Question 5.

735
00:42:34.417 --> 00:42:37.297
Design matchmaking for a 5v5 game.

736
00:42:37.577 --> 00:42:38.942
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:42:39.222 --> 00:42:57.846
Tickets (solo or party) with ratings and region pings go into regional pools; every second a match function forms groups within skill windows that widen with wait time, oldest first, then balances teams by total skill; the allocator assigns a warm server and players get join tokens.

738
00:42:59.196 --> 00:43:00.305
Question 6.

739
00:43:00.585 --> 00:43:03.721
Explain client-side prediction and reconciliation.

740
00:43:04.001 --> 00:43:05.367
Thirty-second answer:

741
00:43:05.647 --> 00:43:20.132
The client applies its own inputs immediately for responsiveness and remembers them; when authoritative state arrives with the last processed input, it resets to that state and replays unacknowledged inputs, smoothing small corrections.

742
00:43:21.482 --> 00:43:22.570
