745
00:43:28.829 --> 00:43:42.866
Verify store receipts server-side, use the store transaction ID as an idempotency key, credit currency through a double-entry ledger, grant entitlements atomically with the debit, and handle refund webhooks with clawbacks.

746
00:43:44.216 --> 00:43:45.240
Question 8.

747
00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:47.525
How do you autoscale game servers?

748
00:43:47.805 --> 00:43:49.171
Thirty-second answer:

749
00:43:49.451 --> 00:44:03.637
Keep a buffer of warm Ready servers per region and mode, add nodes when the buffer drops, drain nodes without active matches when scaling down, pre-scale for launches, and mix reserved or bare-metal baseline with cloud burst.

750
00:44:04.987 --> 00:44:06.054
Question 9.

751
00:44:06.334 --> 00:44:08.382
How do you handle the season-launch spike?

752
00:44:08.662 --> 00:44:10.027
Thirty-second answer:

753
00:44:10.307 --> 00:44:22.958
Pre-download content days before, pre-scale fleets and matchmakers, pre-warm CDNs, flip the season via remote config, protect login with a queue, and watch dashboards with kill switches ready.

754
00:44:24.308 --> 00:44:25.076
Senior.

755
00:44:25.356 --> 00:44:26.401
Question 10.

756
00:44:26.681 --> 00:44:29.433
Explain lag compensation and its trade-offs.

757
00:44:29.713 --> 00:44:31.079
Thirty-second answer:

758
00:44:31.359 --> 00:44:49.919
The server keeps a short history of hitboxes, and on a shot rewinds other players to where the shooter saw them (now minus latency minus interpolation delay), casts the ray, then restores; it makes aiming fair for the shooter but causes died behind cover for the victim, so cap the rewind window.

759
00:44:51.269 --> 00:44:52.399
Question 11.

760
00:44:52.679 --> 00:44:56.327
How do you scale a leaderboard to 50 million players with 'my rank'?

761
00:44:56.607 --> 00:44:57.973
Thirty-second answer:

762
00:44:58.253 --> 00:45:17.879
Exact sorted set for the top 10k; for everyone else maintain a score histogram (bucket counts) updated incrementally, rank about count above plus estimate within bucket and show percentile; or shard by score range with a count index; friends boards computed per request; seasonal keys and archives.

763
00:45:19.229 --> 00:45:20.317
Question 12.

764
00:45:20.597 --> 00:45:23.797
How does TrueSkill differ from Elo, and why would you use it?

765
00:45:24.077 --> 00:45:25.443
Thirty-second answer:

766
00:45:25.723 --> 00:45:40.485
TrueSkill models skill as a Gaussian with mean and uncertainty, supports teams and multi-team placements, updates via Bayesian message passing, and matches on conservative skill, better for team and battle royale games and new players.

767
00:45:41.835 --> 00:45:43.115
Question 13.

768
00:45:43.395 --> 00:45:45.635
Design anti-cheat for a mobile shooter.

769
00:45:45.915 --> 00:45:47.281
Thirty-second answer:

770
00:45:47.561 --> 00:46:05.118
Server authority and validation, interest management to hide unseen enemies, client integrity checks for tampering, emulators and root, server-side statistical models on aim and reaction features, weighted player reports with replay review, delayed ban waves and appeals.

771
00:46:06.468 --> 00:46:07.663
Question 14.

772
00:46:07.943 --> 00:46:10.929
How do you reduce bandwidth for a 100-player match?

773
00:46:11.209 --> 00:46:12.575
Thirty-second answer:

774
00:46:12.855 --> 00:46:26.039
Interest management (spatial grid, visibility), delta compression against acknowledged snapshots, quantisation of positions and angles, priority-based update rates, and sending inputs rather than state upstream.

775
00:46:27.389 --> 00:46:28.285
Architect.

776
00:46:28.565 --> 00:46:29.759
Question 15.

777
00:46:30.039 --> 00:46:34.455
Design the backend for a battle royale game with 3M concurrent players.

778
00:46:34.735 --> 00:46:36.101
Thirty-second answer:

779
00:46:36.381 --> 00:46:50.631
Accounts, social and presence services; regional matchmakers with latency probing and skill windows; an allocator over Agones-style fleets with warm buffers in many regions; authoritative 30 Hz servers with prediction,

780
00:46:50.911 --> 00:47:03.541
lag compensation and interest management; async match-result pipeline to ratings, progression, ledger, leaderboards and anti-cheat; telemetry to Kafka and a lakehouse; live ops config.

781
00:47:04.891 --> 00:47:06.085
Question 16.

782
00:47:06.365 --> 00:47:10.099
How would you design the in-game economy to be exploit-resistant?

783
00:47:10.379 --> 00:47:11.744
Thirty-second answer:

784
00:47:12.024 --> 00:47:29.069
A server-authoritative double-entry ledger with idempotent grants and purchases, separate paid and free currency accounting, limits and velocity anomaly detection, config-driven drop tables on the server, kill switches for items and rollback tooling via reversal entries.

785
00:47:30.419 --> 00:47:31.763
Question 17.

786
00:47:32.043 --> 00:47:34.881
What data platform would you build for a live game?

787
00:47:35.161 --> 00:47:36.526
Thirty-second answer:

788
00:47:36.806 --> 00:47:54.513
Client, server and service events into Kafka; stream processing for live dashboards and fraud; a lakehouse with warehouse marts for KPIs, balance, economy and match quality; ML for MMR, cheat detection, churn and offers; experiment analysis with guardrails.

789
00:47:55.863 --> 00:47:57.036
Question 18.

790
00:47:57.316 --> 00:48:00.452
How do you decide the tick rate and server region footprint?

791
00:48:00.732 --> 00:48:02.097
Thirty-second answer:

792
00:48:02.377 --> 00:48:20.852
Tick rate balances responsiveness against CPU and bandwidth cost per match (competitive modes justify 60 plus Hz); region footprint follows player distribution and RTT targets, with enough population per region to keep queues short, merge regions at low population.

793
00:48:22.202 --> 00:48:23.439
Question 19.

794
00:48:23.719 --> 00:48:25.618
How would you run live ops safely?

795
00:48:25.898 --> 00:48:27.263
Thirty-second answer:

796
00:48:27.543 --> 00:48:41.218
Versioned remote config with targeting, staged rollouts and experiments with guardrail metrics, scheduled content activation, kill switches for items and modes, economy anomaly monitors, and rollback of configs.

797
00:48:42.568 --> 00:48:43.677
Question 20.

798
00:48:43.957 --> 00:48:47.584
Compare a game backend with a ride-hailing or trading backend.

799
00:48:47.864 --> 00:48:49.229
Thirty-second answer:

800
00:48:49.509 --> 00:49:07.877
All match supply and demand in real time (matchmaking ~ dispatch ~ order matching) and need low latency; games uniquely run a continuous authoritative simulation per match with netcode and face adversarial clients, while their economy needs banking-grade ledgers like trading.

801
00:49:09.227 --> 00:49:10.273
Part 13.

802
00:49:10.553 --> 00:49:11.321
Summary.

803
00:49:12.571 --> 00:49:21.189
Authoritative dedicated servers plus client prediction, interpolation and lag compensation make games feel responsive and stay fair.

804
00:49:21.469 --> 00:49:27.869
Matchmaking balances skill (Elo or Glicko or TrueSkill), latency, party makeup and wait time.

805
00:49:28.149 --> 00:49:30.432
Skill windows widen as players wait.

806
00:49:30.712 --> 00:49:35.789
Fleet management keeps warm servers near players and scales for launches and seasons.

807
00:49:36.069 --> 00:49:38.821
Server CPU and egress are the main costs.

808
00:49:39.101 --> 00:49:44.669
Leaderboards use exact sorted sets for the top and histograms or percentiles at scale.

809
00:49:44.949 --> 00:49:47.040
Only server-validated results count.

810
00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:52.102
The economy is a double-entry ledger with idempotent grants.

811
00:49:52.382 --> 00:49:57.118
Anti-cheat is layered: design, client integrity, statistics and reports.

